<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indira Gandhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> (1917–1984) was an Indian politician, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, who served as prime minister from 1966 to 1977 and from 1980 until her death, the only woman to hold the office. She nationalised the banks, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in the war of 1971 and ruled by decree during the Emergency of 1975–1977. She was assassinated by her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> bodyguards after ordering the army into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Indira Nehru was born on 19 November 1917 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, into a Kashmiri Pandit family. Her father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, was a leading figure in the movement for independence from British rule. She was her parents' only surviving child and grew up with her mother, Kamala Nehru, at the Anand Bhavan estate. Her childhood was lonely: her father was often away or in prison, and her mother, frequently ill, died of tuberculosis in February 1936. She studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and at Santiniketan, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, and married Feroze Gandhi, with whom she had two sons, Rajiv and Sanjay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1959, as president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, she played a part in the dissolution of the communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-bahadur-shastri">Lal Bahadur Shastri</a> made her minister of information and broadcasting in 1964, and after his death in January 1966 she defeated Morarji Desai in the party's leadership election. Party bosses and the press at first called her a dumb doll, Goongi goodiya in Hindi. The Congress expelled her in 1969, and her faction, the INC (R), won a landslide in 1971 under the slogan Garibi Hatao (Remove Poverty), an answer to the opposition's Indira Hatao.</p><p>In 1969 she nationalised fourteen major commercial banks. In December 1971 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> intervened in the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, where the Pakistani army's repression had sent about ten million refugees across the border; it ended in Indian victory and the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. India tested a nuclear device in 1974, and Sikkim joined the Indian Union in 1975. After extending the state of emergency twice, she called elections in 1977 and lost her seat as Congress fell from 350 seats to 153. Congress returned in January 1980, and Sanjay died in a plane crash on 23 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> the preacher Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale, whom Congress had helped to prominence, moved into a guest house near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> in 1982 with about 200 armed followers. Indira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was assassinated on 31 October 1984, while chairing the Non-Aligned Movement. World leaders mourned her, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> for the funeral. She had received the Bharat Ratna in 1971, and Henry Kissinger's phrase iron lady became her nickname.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indira Gandhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Congress governed almost without interruption until 1977, under Nehru, Lal Bahadur Shastri and <b>Indira Gandhi</b>, who nationalised the banks in 1969.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In October Prime Minister <b>Indira Gandhi</b> was assassinated by two of her Sikh bodyguards, and thousands of Sikhs were killed in the violence that followed in Delhi and other cities.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Bandung also brought together U Nu, V. K. Krishna Menon, Ho Chi Minh, Zhou Enlai, Norodom Sihanouk, U Thant and a young <b>Indira Gandhi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance to <b>Indira Gandhi</b>’s Emergency of 1975 to 1977 brought rapid growth, Jan Sangh leaders such as Atal Bihari Vajpayee and L. K. Advani rose to national prominence, and the Bharatiya Janata Party was founded in April 1980.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1984 Rakesh Sharma, the first Indian astronaut, used its opening line to describe to Prime Minister <b>Indira Gandhi</b> how India looked from space, and Manmohan Singh quoted the poem at his first press conference as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress parliamentary party elected <b>Indira Gandhi</b> as its leader after the sudden death of Lal Bahadur Shastri.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.45,81.85&amp;year=1960" aria-label="Prayagraj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6717 1228 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Indira Gandhi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7167 1498h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7167 1498h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.45,81.85&amp;year=1960">What was here in 1960? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
